※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=9 diag=none

※ A definition of an operator is a function definition, so it may be generic:
※ one definition for every type a call gives it, made once per set of types like
※ any other.  Two types go through the one definition here.
※
※ And a requirement asks for exactly this.  `pre(T' ⊞ T' → T')` over a type a
※ program defined was unsatisfiable before an operator could be attached to one,
※ because only a built-in type could answer.

type Point = x : u8 ; y : u8
type Pair = a : u8 ; b : u8

fn `+`(p: T', q: T') → T':
    p

fn `⍴`(p: Point) → u64:
    ⎕widen(p.x, ⌜u64⌝) + ⎕widen(p.y, ⌜u64⌝)

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    let p: Point = Point(.x ← 1u8, .y ← 2u8)
    let q: Pair = Pair(.a ← 5u8, .b ← 6u8)
    let r: Point = p + p
    let s: Pair = q + q
    ⎕narrow(⍴p ⊞ ⎕widen(r.x ⊞ s.a, ⌜u64⌝), ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
